High-Performance Parallel Sparse Matrix-Matrix Multiplication (SpMM)
Couurse: CSE 3100 Systems Programming
Members: Denae-Raenn Green, Harold Vilchez, & Jesse Hubicki

1. Objective and Scope
  In this project we replaced dense 2D array storage with a flat, spave-efficient Coordinate (COO)
  sparse-matrix representation and implemented two Matrix-Matrix multiplicatio engines for real-world
  sparse matrices streamed from the NIST/SuiteSparse Matrix Market ecosystem: a single-threaded sequential baseline 
  (spam_sequential) serving as the mathematical ground truth, and a multi-threaded parallel engine (spam_parallel)
  built on the POSIX Threads API, with (1) correctness protocol that verifies the parallel engine against the sequential
  basline cell-by-cell at a floating-point tolerance of EPSILOn = 1e-7 across thread counts 1, 2, 4, 8, and 16 and (2)
  an empiral performance study measuring speedup on ten real-world SuiteSparse matrices on two distinct hardware patforms,
  a university lab workstation and a personal laptp.

